Tideway uses Python's standard library for runtime code.

The MIT license covers Tideway application code only. Model weights, base-model
architectures, runtimes and optional tools retain the licenses of their authors.

Optional models and inference:

* TideLM is a Tideway-trained LoRA adapter for Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507,
  converted to 4-bit MLX by mlx-community. The base model is Apache-2.0.
  Upstream: https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507
  Conversion: https://huggingface.co/mlx-community/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-4bit
  License copy: licenses/Qwen3-Apache-2.0.txt
* TideVision uses mlx-community/Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct-4bit, derived from
  Qwen/Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct, without a Tideway fine-tune. The upstream
  Qwen RESEARCH LICENSE AGREEMENT permits non-commercial research/evaluation;
  commercial use requires a separate license. Redistribution carries additional
  notice and license obligations. No vision weights are included in this package.
  Qwen is licensed under the Qwen RESEARCH LICENSE AGREEMENT,
  Copyright (c) Alibaba Cloud. All Rights Reserved.
  Upstream: https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct
  Conversion: https://huggingface.co/mlx-community/Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct-4bit
  License copy: licenses/Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Research.txt
* MLX, mlx-lm and mlx-vlm are optional Apple Silicon inference/training tools.
  MLX and mlx-lm use MIT; mlx-vlm uses MIT. Their upstream notices remain
  applicable when installed or redistributed. No runtime/weights are bundled.
  https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx
  https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx-lm
  https://github.com/Blaizzy/mlx-vlm
* Pillow (HPND) is optional for photo processing and slide image crops.
  https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow/blob/main/LICENSE
* The optional `scripts/local_demo_model.py` helper provisions compatible GGUF
  text models separately. `compact` uses Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct-GGUF
  (Q4_K_M); `qwen3-4b` uses unsloth/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-GGUF (Q4_K_M),
  derived from Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507. Both model cards identify Apache-2.0.
  These are third-party models/conversions, not Tideway-trained adapters, and
  they do not inherit the released TideLM MLX evaluation scores.
  https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct-GGUF
  https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-GGUF
  https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507
  Apache-2.0 license copy: licenses/Qwen3-Apache-2.0.txt
* The helper downloads an optional checksum-pinned llama.cpp native runtime
  from ggml-org/llama.cpp release b11678. llama.cpp is MIT; downloaded runtime
  archives can contain additional component notices that remain applicable.
  The helper records model/runtime provenance and supports an explicit external
  cache directory. No GGUF weights, runtime executables, DLLs or dylibs are
  included in the source/submission package.
  https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp
  https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/LICENSE

Build and demo tooling:

* Playwright (Apache-2.0) and a locally installed Chrome/Chromium/Edge are used
  for the optional recording script. Neither is bundled in the source package.
* FFmpeg encodes the demo. The encoder's own build-specific license applies;
  its binary is not redistributed here. The demo credits synthetic narration
  generated locally using the installed Windows System.Speech voice.

Model scope and reproducibility are documented in docs/MODEL_CARD.md.

Optional verification tooling:

* The optional browser verification script (`scripts/capture_screens.mjs`)
  uses Node.js built-in WebSocket support and an installed Chromium/Chrome
  browser. These tools are not bundled in the application runtime and remain
  subject to their own licenses and installation terms.
* The optional AI adapter sends consented requests to the OpenAI Responses API.
  OpenAI services, models, SDK/API terms, and retention policies are external
  to this repository. No API key or provider asset is included here.

The supplied synthetic records and generated screenshots are fictional demo
material authored for this project. Do not replace them with private customer
data without reviewing consent, licensing, and applicable law.

Contest-upgrade evaluation and visual QA:

* The core evaluation and linked-delay scripts use the Python standard library.
  Synthetic fixtures and newly generated reports are project-authored evidence.
* Optional PDF inspection uses pypdf and PyMuPDF, and photo/layout tests use
  Pillow. `requirements-evaluation.txt` pins the QA environment used here.
  These packages are installed separately, retain their upstream license terms,
  and are not bundled in the source archive. No video was recorded or encoded
  during the contest-upgrade workflow.

